# @domain — Active Directory client authentication (plan/18).
#
# TWO ATOMS, and everything else resolves. sssd's own closure is large — openldap, cyrus-sasl,
# ding-libs, p11-kit, virtual/ldb, net-dns/bind — and none of it is listed here, for the reason
# @installer states: naming transitive deps in a set is how a set stops describing intent and
# starts duplicating the resolver.
#
# UNLIKE @installer, this set is named by EVERY profile. That is deliberate. An image cannot
# install software after the fact — there is no Portage on the target, by design (plan/06) — so
# "this machine can be joined to a domain later" has to mean "the client is already on it".
# Joining then writes configuration and a keytab into the /etc overlay and enables one unit; it
# installs nothing. See plan/18 §2 for the full list of what ships versus what a join writes.
#
# The unjoined case is the one that matters, and it is asserted rather than assumed: with no
# sssd socket, nss_sss returns unavailable and the next NSS module answers, and pam_sss returns
# PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL and the stack falls through to pam_unix. sssd.service is disabled in the
# vendor preset AND carries ConditionPathExists=/etc/sssd/sssd.conf, because on this system a
# unit that starts and fails is not a warning — systemd-boot-check-no-failures gates
# boot-complete.target, so it is a failed boot and an automatic rollback (plan/18 §5.1).
#
# sys-auth/sssd — stable amd64, so no keyword exception. Built with USE=-samba: that flag gates
# only the PAC responder and GPO support and would pull net-fs/samba[winbind]; the `ad` provider
# itself is unconditional, and with access_provider=permit there are no GPOs to evaluate.
sys-auth/sssd
# app-crypt/adcli — stable amd64. The join tool: creates the computer account over LDAP/GSSAPI
# and writes the host keytab. ~200 KiB, and its only dependencies (mit-krb5, openldap[sasl]) are
# ones sssd already requires. This is what realmd would have been used for; realmd is not in the
# Gentoo tree at all, and it is only a d-bus wrapper around adcli plus config generation — which
# is what usr/bin/<distro>-domain does (plan/18 §1, §4).
app-crypt/adcli
